# ⚠️  SHELL SCRIPTS MUST KEEP LF ENDINGS, EVEN ON WINDOWS.
#
# Git on Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout. A shell script with CRLF endings fails inside a Linux container in
# a way that reads as nonsense, because the \r becomes part of the interpreter
# path and of every argument:
#
#     /usr/bin/env: 'sh\r': No such file or directory
#     : not founde 12: start.sh
#
# backend/start.sh is the container entrypoint, so this would stop the backend
# from booting at all — on a clean clone, on someone else's machine, with an
# error pointing nowhere near the cause.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning: these are read by Linux tooling inside containers.
Dockerfile   text eol=lf
*.dockerfile text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf
*.yml        text eol=lf
*.yaml       text eol=lf

# Let Git normalise everything else, and never touch binaries.
* text=auto
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.tif  binary
*.tiff binary
*.dcm  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.ico  binary
